# 1. COM add-in communication with the Bot

## Recommended transport: Windows Named Pipes

The Word/Outlook COM add-in and Bot are both native Windows processes running on the same computer. Named Pipes are a good match because they:

- Do not expose a TCP port.
- Can be limited to the currently signed-in Windows user.
- Work between a .NET Framework COM add-in and a modern .NET Bot.
- Support request/response communication.
- Are faster and simpler than routing local paths through the backend.

.NET provides `NamedPipeServerStream` and `NamedPipeClientStream` specifically for local inter-process communication. Pipe security can restrict access to the intended Windows user. [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.pipes.namedpipeserverstream?view=net-10.0\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Example pipe:

```text
\\.\pipe\Corto.DocumentsBot.{WindowsUserSid}
```

Including the Windows user SID prevents different signed-in Windows users from accidentally connecting to one another’s Bot instance.

## COM add-in flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Office as Word/Outlook add-in
    participant Bot as Documents Bot
    participant API as Corto backend

    Office->>Bot: documentLocation
    Bot-->>Office: Command accepted
    Bot->>Bot: Resolve matter mapping
    Bot->>API: Check metadata and permission
    Bot->>API: Upload or register document
    API-->>Bot: New version confirmed
    Bot-->>Office: Sync completed
```

The add-in sends a small JSON message, not the document contents:

```json
{
  "messageType": "documentLocation",
  "protocolVersion": 1,
  "commandId": "01J...",
  "accountId": "account-123",
  "matterId": "matter-456",
  "sourceApplication": "Word",
  "documentPath": "C:\\Matters\\Smith\\Contract.docx",
  "occurredAtUtc": "2026-10-01T05:50:00Z"
}
```

The Bot responds immediately:

```json
{
  "commandId": "01J...",
  "accepted": true,
  "status": "Queued"
}
```

“Accepted” should mean that the command has been stored durably in SQLite—not that the upload has already finished.

## Add-in responsibilities

The COM add-in should:

- Detect the relevant Word or Outlook action.
- Obtain the local document path.
- Send `documentLocation` to the Bot.
- Display basic states such as “queued,” “syncing,” or “Bot unavailable.”
- Start the installed Bot and retry if it is not running.

It should not:

- Upload directly to S3.
- Store permanent AWS credentials.
- Implement conflict resolution.
- Maintain its own copy of the synchronization queue.
- Decide whether an S3 object should overwrite another version.

## Bot handling

After receiving `documentLocation`, the Bot should:

1. Validate the message and local path.
2. Check the authenticated user and account.
3. Find an existing local-folder-to-matter mapping.
4. If no mapping exists, walk up the directory tree and compare it with stored folder snapshots.
5. Create a suggested matter-folder mapping if appropriate.
6. Store the operation in the durable queue.
7. Upload when the file is stable and no longer being written.
8. Notify the add-in, PWA and backend of the result.

