# 2. PWA communication with the Bot

## Recommended transport: secure localhost API

The Bot hosts a small API bound only to loopback:

```text
https://127.0.0.1:<configured-port>
wss://127.0.0.1:<configured-port>/events
```

Suggested usage:

| Channel | Purpose |
|---|---|
| REST/HTTP | Health check and one-off commands |
| WebSocket | Sync progress, status changes and notifications |
| Custom URI | Start or bring the Bot to the foreground |
| Backend SignalR | Remote changes and multi-user coordination |

Example endpoints:

```text
GET  /api/v1/status
POST /api/v1/commands/matter-folder-import
POST /api/v1/folder-picker/open
GET  /api/v1/sync-jobs/{jobId}
WSS  /api/v1/events
```

## PWA flow

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant PWA as Corto PWA
    participant Bot as Documents Bot
    participant API as Corto backend

    PWA->>Bot: Check status
    Bot-->>PWA: Running + authenticated user
    PWA->>Bot: matterFolderImport command
    Bot->>Bot: Open native folder picker
    Bot-->>PWA: Folder selected
    Bot->>API: Validate matter and provider
    Bot->>Bot: Save mapping and start sync
    Bot-->>PWA: Progress events
```

Example PWA command:

```json
{
  "messageType": "matterFolderImport",
  "protocolVersion": 1,
  "commandId": "01J...",
  "accountId": "account-123",
  "matterId": "matter-456",
  "deviceId": "device-789"
}
```

The PWA should generally not supply a Windows path. Browsers do not reliably expose full native filesystem paths. Instead, the Bot should open the native Windows folder picker and return a safe display value such as:

```json
{
  "commandId": "01J...",
  "status": "FolderSelected",
  "folderDisplayName": "Smith",
  "mappingId": "mapping-222"
}
```

The PWA does not need to receive the complete path unless there is a confirmed business requirement.

## Starting the Bot

Register a Windows custom protocol during installation:

```text
corto-documents://open
corto-documents://matter/account-123/matter-456
```

If the health check fails, the PWA can invoke the protocol and retry the localhost connection.

The protocol should contain only identifiers and a short-lived token. It must never include:

- Access tokens
- AWS credentials
- Document contents
- Sensitive full paths

## Browser consideration

An HTTPS PWA communicating with localhost needs careful validation across supported browsers and corporate policies. Browsers are strengthening local-network access controls, and insecure WebSockets should not be opened from secure pages. [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Defenses/Local_network_access?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Therefore, a technical proof of concept should test:

- Chrome and Edge
- Installed PWA and ordinary browser-tab modes
- Corporate browser policies
- CORS and origin validation
- Local-network permission prompts
- Local certificate deployment
- WebSocket reconnect
- Custom protocol launching

