# 3. PWA communication options

| Option | Advantages | Limitations | Recommendation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Localhost API/WebSocket | Fast; knows the PWA and Bot are on the same machine; good for folder picker and live progress | Certificate, CORS and browser-policy complexity | Recommended for same-machine interaction, subject to POC |
| PWA → backend → Bot via SignalR | Central authentication; works without opening a local server; supports device targeting | Requires internet; more latency; local paths should not pass through backend | Recommended as fallback and for remote events |
| Custom URI protocol | Can start the Bot and pass a small command | Mostly one-way; difficult to return progress | Use only for launching/activation |
| Browser extension/native messaging | Strong browser-to-native integration | Requires extension installation and browser-specific deployment | Avoid unless localhost is blocked |
| Shared files/database | Simple prototype | Race conditions, weak notification and security model | Not recommended |

## Recommended hybrid

1. Use **localhost REST/WebSocket** for same-device operations.
2. Use a **custom URI** to start the Bot.
3. Use **backend SignalR** for server document changes and multi-user coordination.
4. Fall back to backend routing if browser security policy blocks direct localhost communication.

ASP.NET Core SignalR has both JavaScript and .NET clients and supports automatic reconnection when explicitly configured. [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/signalr/dotnet-client?view=aspnetcore-10.0\&utm_source=chatgpt.com)

