# 5. Recommended Bot project type

## Primary recommendation

In Visual Studio, create:

> **Windows Forms App (.NET)**

Do not select:

> Windows Forms App (.NET Framework)

The Bot can use modern .NET even if the COM add-in uses .NET Framework, because they communicate through a versioned JSON Named Pipe protocol rather than sharing runtime-specific objects.

## Suggested solution structure

```text
Corto.DocumentsBot.sln
│
├── Corto.DocumentsBot.App
│   └── Windows Forms tray application and composition root
│
├── Corto.DocumentsBot.Core
│   └── Sync rules, commands, models and interfaces
│
├── Corto.DocumentsBot.Infrastructure
│   └── SQLite, filesystem, backend API, S3 transfer and SignalR
│
├── Corto.DocumentsBot.Ipc
│   └── Named Pipes, localhost API and protocol contracts
│
├── Corto.DocumentsBot.Contracts
│   └── Versioned DTOs shared with the COM add-in where practical
│
└── Corto.DocumentsBot.Tests
    └── Unit, integration and synchronization tests
```

Inside the WinForms process, use the .NET Generic Host and `BackgroundService` implementations for:

- Filesystem monitoring
- Queue processing
- Server event listening
- Retry processing
- Periodic reconciliation
- Local IPC hosting

This gives the team Worker Service-style architecture while still providing a tray icon and running within the signed-in user’s Windows session.

## Why not only a Windows Service?

A Windows Service is suitable for a long-running headless workload, but it does not naturally belong to the interactive user desktop. Microsoft’s guidance treats Worker Services as long-running headless workloads, and Windows services require careful handling because they may run without any user logged in and potentially under a more privileged account. [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/apps/desktop/modernize/dotnet/background-tasks?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For this project, a Windows Service complicates:

- Per-user Corto authentication
- Native folder picker
- System-tray UI
- Access to mapped drives
- Office add-in communication
- Multiple Windows users on the same computer

Use a separate Windows Service only if there is a confirmed requirement to synchronize:

- Before a user signs in
- While nobody is logged into Windows
- Machine-wide rather than per user

That would require two executables: a Windows Service plus a per-user tray UI, with another secure IPC boundary between them.

## Other project options

| Project type | When it makes sense | Assessment |
|---|---|---|
| WinForms App (.NET) | Tray app with modest settings/status UI | Best baseline |
| WPF App (.NET) | Richer Windows UI is required | Good alternative |
| Electron | React reuse and cross-platform support are important | Higher memory and packaging overhead |
| Worker Service only | Completely headless background processing | Not suitable for tray/native interaction |
| Windows Service | Must sync without a logged-in user | Adds substantial complexity |
| Windows App SDK/WinUI | Modern Windows-only UI is a major requirement | Possible, but unnecessary for initial sync scope |

